; 18-choose-the-theory-to-load.kurt
;
; Choose the theory to load
;
; | Theory | Main contents |
; |---|---|
; | `prop` | `not`, `or`, `iff`, contradiction, propositional introduction/elimination rules |
; | `logic` | `forall`, `exists`, conditioned quantifier rules; loads `prop` |
; | `equality` | `=`, `!=`/`≠`, reflexivity and substitution; loads `prop` |
; | `set` | membership, subsets, separation, union/intersection, products, tuples, mappings |
; | `numbers` | exact calculation bindings, algebraic laws, order, powers, factorial |
; | `natural` | natural numbers: induction, the predecessor, well-ordering, even and odd, divisibility `∣`, `coprime` |
; | `integer` | the integers `Int` |
; | `rational` | the rationals `Rat`, every positive rational in lowest terms |
; | `analysis` | absolute value, finite sums, extrema, suprema/infima, limits |
; | `group` | reusable group structure and derived group theorems |
; | `field` | a field `K` with `+`, `·`, `-`, `inv`, `0`, `1`: every axiom for the elements of `K`, subtraction |
; | `vectorspace` | vector spaces over `K`: `vectorspace($V)`, the axioms for its elements |
; | `matrix` | vectors and matrices as literals, computed exactly with `calc on` (lesson 25) |
;
; Numbers or structures: `numbers` (and `natural`, `integer`, `rational`, `analysis`, which load
; it) is about numbers -- its laws hold for everything written with `+`, `*`, ... For linear
; algebra, use a structure that declares its own operators, like `field` or
; `vectorspace`, not together with `numbers`: a symbol is declared by one file only, and
; loading two files that both declare `+` is an error (doc/kurt-doc.md, `load`). `group` takes
; its operation as an argument, so it goes with either. (`numbers` used to be called `arith`.)
;
; Dependencies are loaded transitively. For example, `load natural` brings in `set`, `logic`,
; `equality`, `prop`, and `numbers` as required. Use `kurt --deps FILE` to inspect the actual tree.
;
; The packaged `modal` file is an experimental example, not a mature theory. It demonstrates
; box/diamond syntax and several sound modal axiom schemata, but it is not a complete proof system
; for normal modal logic K or T because Kurt cannot yet express the necessitation rule.
